---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="About" description="About John Rick Manzanares, a Filipino mathematician and computational researcher.">
  <header class="page-hero shell">
    <p class="kicker">About</p>
    <h1>Becoming, always.</h1>
    <p class="page-intro">
      <em>Banyuhay</em> means metamorphosis or transformation in Filipino. The word has stayed with me because
      my path through mathematics has changed form several times while keeping the same underlying curiosity.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell about-grid">
      <img class="portrait" src="/assets/JohnRick.jpg" alt="John Rick Manzanares" loading="lazy" />

      <div class="about-story">
        <p>
          I am John Rick Manzanares, a Filipino mathematician and computational researcher from Baguio City
          in the northern Philippines. I studied mathematics at the University of the Philippines Baguio
          after attending Philippine Science High School, Cordillera Administrative Region Campus.
        </p>
        <p>
          My early interests were closer to differential geometry. The direction changed when I encountered
          topological data analysis and began to see topology not only as an abstract language, but also as a
          way to ask quantitative questions about data and structure.
        </p>
        <p>
          My master’s work studied centrality measures built from homology. Later projects brought topology
          into electrocardiogram classification, while other work moved toward computer vision and scientific
          data analysis. Each change added another way to think about the same broad question: what information
          about structure should be preserved when a complicated object is reduced to something we can compute with?
        </p>
        <p>
          I am now a Marie Skłodowska-Curie doctoral fellow working in Poland. My doctoral research studies
          bone microstructure using topological data analysis, computational geometry, scientific computing,
          and machine learning.
        </p>
        <p>
          Teaching and public software are also part of how I think about research. I prefer explanations that
          define their objects carefully, code that can be inspected rather than treated as a black box, and
          tools that remain useful after a single experiment is over.
        </p>

        <ul class="fact-list">
          <li><strong>Research</strong><span>Topology, geometry, scientific computing, machine learning</span></li>
          <li><strong>Current focus</strong><span>Bone microstructure and computational descriptors</span></li>
          <li><strong>From</strong><span>Baguio City, Philippines</span></li>
          <li><strong>Based in</strong><span>Poland</span></li>
          <li><strong>Code</strong><span>Python, Rust, Julia, R, and whatever else the problem genuinely requires</span></li>
        </ul>

        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button" href="mailto:jdolormanzanares@impan.pl">Email me</a>
          <a class="button secondary" href="https://github.com/jhnrckmnznrs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section banyuhay-band">
    <div class="shell banyuhay-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">Why Banyuhay</p>
        <p class="banyuhay-word">Change is not a detour.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="banyuhay-copy">
        <p>
          Research rarely develops as a straight line. A mathematical interest becomes a thesis, a thesis
          becomes software, a computational problem exposes a new theorem, and a scientific application changes
          the questions worth asking.
        </p>
        <p>
          I want this website to show that process rather than flatten it into a list of positions and outputs.
          The finished papers matter, but so do the explanations, failed directions, reusable code, teaching
          material, and questions that are still open.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
